# Branch groups: amenability, free subgroups, growth (Kourovka Notebook Problem 15.14)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 15.14 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z. Šunić): do there exist finitely
generated branch groups (see 15.12) (a) that are non-amenable, (b) that are non-amenable and do not
contain the free group `F₂`, (c) that contain `F₂`, (d) that have exponential growth? Parts (a),
(c), (d) are in the Archive of Solved Problems; part (b) is marked solved in the main list.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real
public import Mathlib.Geometry.Group.WordMetric
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AmenableGroup
public import MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitelyGeneratedBranchGroupsKourovka15Problem14

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitelyGeneratedBranchGroupsKourovka15Problem14Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 15.14 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z.
Šunić); parts (a), (c), (d) in the Archive of Solved Problems, part (b) in the main list.
UnsolvedMath record UM-00701102 (`KOU-15.14`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Branch groups follow Problem 15.12: a faithful, level-transitive action by automorphisms on a rooted
tree in which every rigid level stabilizer (the subgroup generated by the rigid vertex stabilizers
of the vertices of one level, a rigid vertex stabilizer consisting of the elements whose support
lies in the subtree below the vertex) has finite index. Level-transitivity forces the tree to be
spherically homogeneous, so the tree is modelled as the words whose `i`-th letter is below `m i`,
with all branching indices `m i ≥ 2` as in Grigorchuk's setting (the survey cited in 15.12); tree
automorphisms are the permutations of the vertices preserving the prefix (ancestor) order in both
directions. This is the geometric definition of L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk and Z. Šunić, Branch
groups, Handbook of Algebra 3 (2003), arXiv:math/0510294 (Definition 1.13, on the trees of words
with all `m i ≥ 2` of Section 1.2), which their open-problems chapter uses for its versions of
Problems 15.14 (b), (c), 15.15, 15.17 and 15.18 (Questions 20, 15, 14, 10, 9 and 11); Kionke and
Schesler (Section 2.1) and Bondarenko (Section 2) use the same definition. Amenability is
MathlibExt's Følner-condition `IsAmenable`. Growth rates follow Problem 14.7: `ω(G, P) = limsup
β_P(n)^(1/n)`, where `β_P(n)` counts the elements of word length at most `n` for a finite generating
family `P`; the growth type does not depend on the generating set, and the definitions below
quantify over every finite generating family. Containing `F₂` means an injective homomorphism from
the free group of rank `2`.
-/

/-- **Kourovka Problem 15.14(b): non-amenable branch groups without free subgroups.** The
statement that there exists a finitely generated branch group that is not amenable and contains
no free subgroup of rank $2$. -/
def fgNonamenableBranchGroupWithoutFreeSubgroup : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧ IsBranchGroup G ∧ ¬ IsAmenable G ∧
    ¬ ContainsFreeGroupRankTwo G

/--
**Non-amenable branch groups without free subgroups exist (Kourovka 15.14(b)).** There is a
finitely generated branch group that is not amenable and contains no free subgroup of rank $2$.

The Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 15.14(b) solved: Kionke and Schesler constructed
finitely generated non-amenable torsion branch groups, and a torsion group contains no free
subgroup of rank $2$.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in
Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 15.14 (L.
Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z. Šunić); parts (a), (c), (d) in the Archive of Solved Problems, part
(b) in the main list; resolution: S. Sidki and J. S. Wilson, Free subgroups of branch groups, Arch.
Math. 80 (2003), no. 5, 458-463 (parts a, c, d); S. Kionke and E. Schesler, Realizing residually
finite groups as subgroups of branch groups, Bull. London Math. Soc. 56 (2024), no. 2, 536-550,
arXiv:2212.07165 (part b), Kourovka Archive, Problem 15.14 (a), (c), (d): such groups do exist
(Sidki and Wilson's finitely generated branch groups contain free subgroups of rank 2, the positive
answer recorded under Question 15 of Bartholdi, Grigorchuk and Šunić, Branch groups); Kourovka
Notebook (21st issue), Problem 15.14 (b), and Kionke and Schesler, Corollary 1.2: there is a
finitely generated non-amenable torsion branch group, https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07165.
-/
public theorem_wanted fgNonamenableBranchGroupWithoutFreeSubgroup_holds :
    fgNonamenableBranchGroupWithoutFreeSubgroup

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitelyGeneratedBranchGroupsKourovka15Problem14Wanted
